PSGen 1.1 (Karol Kolodziej) -- the subset linked into runDipole
=================================================================

This directory is ASSEMBLED, not authored -- same convention as ../../PSGen and
../../../decay/PSGen. It holds the files PSGen generates for 'g g -> z h g' plus
the runtime routines those files actually reach. THIS IS THE FIRST 3-FINAL-LEG
PSGen instance in this repository (the existing sets are 2- and 4-body).

Local edits: one vendored file (inprms_ps.f) and one data-file addition shared
with every study (common/PSGen_1.1/code_generation/genps.dat) -- both described
in full below.


PSGen could not find this topology on its own
-----------------------------------------------

Regenerating this set is NOT a routine "change PSGen.f:36 and `make code`" the
way ../../PSGen and ../../../decay/PSGen were. With itchnl=0 and igchnl=0, 1 or
2 (all three tried), PSGen's own automatic channel search produced `nkin=0` --
an EMPTY kinschnl.f, not an inefficient one.

The mechanism (traced in genps.f, not guessed from genps.dat alone): for a
3-final-leg process, genps.f loops over every ACTIVE (non-"0"-prefixed) row of
genps.dat and counts a match (`iv`) whenever TWO of the final-state particle
names both appear in that row's particle list. Only matched rows (`iv > 0`) ever
get a channel built. read_process.f correctly detects the lone final-state
gluon and forces igchnl=2 regardless of PSGen.f's own setting (see
read_process.f's "Check for a photon or gluon in the final state" block), and
genps.f's `nfsp==3 .and. igind2>nispt` branch correctly looks for a genps.dat
match among the OTHER two final particles -- {Z, H} here. But no active row in
the vendored genps.dat lists both `z` and `h` together (the only place they
co-occur, `0 z z h : hzz` / `0 z z h h : hhzz`, are `0`-prefixed comment rows
under "Boson interaction vertices", which genps.f's reader explicitly skips).
So `iv` stays 0 and no channel is ever built -- not a flag-tuning problem, a
genuine gap in the tool's vocabulary: PSGen's channel builder needs the
"everything but the radiated boson" residue to match a TABULATED resonance or
radiation pattern, and Z H isn't the decay product of anything in the Standard
Model, so no such pattern could exist.

THE FIX: one technical, non-physical row added to
common/PSGen_1.1/code_generation/genps.dat (shared, permanent, and inert for
every other process -- no other final state in this repository's history has
both z and h):

    2 z h : mt,zero

This reuses `mt`/`zero`, already declared everywhere in the toolchain, purely as
an available mass/width pair -- top plays no physics role here. `,zero` for the
width is genps.dat's own established convention for a flat/non-resonant mapping
(every physical entry in the file already has a `,zero` variant alongside its
`,gamX` one), so this is squarely within the tool's existing design, not a
logic change. With this row present, PSGen's existing `nfsp==3` branch builds
exactly one channel:

    kin3_2(3,4,mt,zero,5,...)

-- legs 3,4 (Z,H) paired under the technical invariant mass, leg 5 (the gluon)
recoiling against the pair. The Breit-Wigner-style mapping (mapckk) is an exact
reparametrization of the invariant-mass integration variable for ANY positive
technical mass, so this is a parametrization choice, not a physics input --
see genps.dat's own comment at the new row, and inprms_ps.f's header below.
`mt = 173 GeV` sits below the physical Z+H threshold (216.4 GeV), which costs
VEGAS sampling efficiency, not correctness; revisit only if convergence needs
it (a later, better choice of technical mass -- or width -- is a one-line
genps.dat edit, not a structural change).


Regenerating
------------

    cd ../../../common/PSGen_1.1/code_generation
    #   PSGen.f:36     process='g g -> z h g'
    #                  itchnl=0  igchnl=0 (read_process.f overrides to 2 anyway)
    #                  iquadp=0  nicknm='auto'
    #   proc_name.f    proc='' -- a NON-BLANK string there silently overrides
    #                  the process in PSGen.f. This bit the decay build once;
    #                  it also bit this regeneration once, from a leftover
    #                  'u u~ -> e+ e-' in a scratch copy -- checked and fixed
    #                  before trusting the output.
    #   genps.dat      the `2 z h : mt,zero` row above is ALREADY THERE,
    #                  committed -- do not re-add it.
    make code

RESTORE PSGen.f:36 AND proc_name.f:proc AFTERWARDS (`git checkout --
common/PSGen_1.1/code_generation`) -- same rule as the other two sets. genps.dat's
new row is NOT reverted; it is permanent, shared infrastructure.

emits, in code_generation/: kinparams.f nkin_decl.f fourmom.f kincls.f
kinschnl.f process_nm.dat (plus engymom.f tchcalls.f kingchnl.f kinkk1.f,
unused: itchnl=igchnl=0 as far as PSGen.f is concerned -- read_process.f's
internal override to igchnl=2 does not change which files come out, only which
channel gets built).

Copy the first six here, then re-copy the runtime set listed below. Read the
new kinschnl.f first: with the genps.dat fix above it names exactly one
routine, kin3_2 -- that is what decided the runtime set below, the same way
../../PSGen/README.psgen's kinschnl.f decided kin2_2 alone was needed there.


What was generated
-------------------

nexp = 5, nfsp = 3 (Z, H, g)  ->  psgen_ndim = 3*nfsp - 4 = 5 random numbers
nkin = 1 channel:

    1        kin3_2(3,4,mt,zero,5)      {Z,H} technical pair, g recoils

ONE CHANNEL, same as ../../PSGen's Born case, but for a structurally different
reason: there the final state needed NO channel at all (kin2_2, direct 2-body
production, no invariant-mass sampling). Here a channel is structurally
required -- three final particles cannot be parametrized without SOME recursive
grouping -- but there is only one way to group them (pair the two that have no
physical resonance under a technical propagator, radiate the third), so
multichannel sampling still has nothing to choose between.

The channel dimension in the eventual integrand therefore still always
resolves to 1, kept for the same reason ../../PSGen's does: so the integrand
shape does not have to special-case a single-channel process.

kin3_2 samples the {Z,H} invariant mass via mapckk (around the technical
mt/zero "propagator", bounded below by sqsc and below by mZ+mH), builds the
pair in its own rest frame via kinff, builds the pair-vs-gluon split at the
full CM energy via a second kinff call, then boosts the pair's two momenta
into the frame the gluon recoils in. This is the SAME kinff routine
../../PSGen's kin2_2 already uses, called twice instead of once.


The linked set
--------------

    generated  kinparams fourmom kincls kinschnl process_nm.dat
    modules    inprms_ps qrkinds kincuts psnrmtest ntdisc drivekk prtinds
    setup      qp_masses define_cuts set_indices cutinfo impcuts logo_ps
               print_masses param_trans
    kinematics kinin kinin_qp kinff kinff_qp kin3_2 mapckk mapc_qp boost boost_qp
    dispatch   kinschnl kincls psncls psgen
    include'd  kin_tests.f  process_nm.dat

NEW RELATIVE TO ../../PSGen's SET: mapckk.f, mapc_qp.f, boost.f, boost_qp.f,
kin3_2.f (replacing kin2_2.f, not needed here -- kin3_2 calls kinff directly,
the same routine kin2_2 itself called, so kin2_2 is not a dependency of
kin3_2). ../../PSGen's README explicitly listed mapckk/boost as NOT copied
there, "since kin2_2 is self-contained"; this is exactly the case where they
ARE needed, for the reason stated there.

The `_qp` twins are dead at iqprec = 0 but referenced at compile time, so they
link -- same as ../../PSGen. Object order in the makefile IS the build order;
there is no dependency tracking.

NOT copied, same reasoning as ../../PSGen:
    carlos.f cs_sect.f PSGen_test_mpi.f ranlux.f kin2_2.f
    (every OTHER kinN_*.f in PSGen_1.1/mc_computation -- other multiplicities)


Local edits
-----------

inprms_ps.f (copied from ../../../common/PSGen_1.1/mc_computation/, the
pristine upstream, then edited -- NOT from ../../PSGen/inprms_ps.f, though the
edit pattern is the same one that file already documents):

  * mz/gamz/mw/gamw set to ../input.input's values (91.188/2.441404/80.419/
    2.0476), matching ../../PSGen/inprms_ps.f exactly. mZ is LOAD-BEARING (an
    external on-shell leg; psgen_init REFUSES TO RUN on a mismatch). mW is
    inert (no W in this final state).

  * mt, gamt LEFT UNCHANGED FROM UPSTREAM (173.0/1.5) -- and unlike
    ../../PSGen's inprms_ps.f, this is not simply "unused elsewhere": mt is
    now this build's OWN technical z-h pairing mass (see above), so leaving it
    at the upstream default rather than picking a new value is itself the
    choice being made, not an oversight.

  * `sqsc` promoted from parameter to variable, same edit as ../../PSGen, but
    NOT inert here: kin3_2 DOES link mapckk, so sqsc genuinely sets the lower
    edge of the {Z,H} invariant-mass window sampled. Left at the upstream
    default (1e-8) since the real lower edge is set by mZ+mH regardless.

No other vendored file is modified.


Verification (Stage 3 of onshell/dipole_standalone's build order)
-------------------------------------------------------------------

../checks/check_psgen3body.f90 calls psgen_init/psgen_event at 7 hand-picked
points (fixed, not random, so a failure is reproducible) and checks, at each:

    p3^2 = mZ^2, p4^2 = mH^2, p5^2 = 0            (on-shell, +--- metric)
    sum(p_in) = sum(p_out)                        (momentum conservation)

Result: 7/7 passed, all four quantities agreeing to ~1e-9 relative or better
(floating-point noise at the GeV^2/GeV scale of this process) -- see the
build's own progress record for the full printout. This is the same
verification ../../PSGen/README.psgen's "verification step 4" describes for
the 2-body case, done here for the first time at nfsp=3.

Not yet checked: PSGen's own im_test/ip_test internal consistency flags
(left at 0 in psgen_interface.f90, same as ../../kp_sa -- cheap to
switch on from a throwaway driver if the kinematics are ever under suspicion
again) and VEGAS-level convergence of the actual subtracted integrand, which
is a later stage of this build (R/sum(D) -> 1 in the soft and collinear
limits), not this one.
